  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The SQL Server event-store suites provision from the DDL the package ships rather than from
      hand-written copies. Linking the file in (instead of duplicating it) is what keeps the two from
      drifting: the copies previously diverged and every append in four suites failed with
      "Invalid column name 'TenantId'". It also means the shipped script is exercised at all — while
      the suites built their own schema, nothing tested the one consumers actually run.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.EventSourcing.SqlServer\Scripts\001_CreateEventStoreSchema.sql" Link="Schema\001_CreateEventStoreSchema.sql" />
    <!--
      The tenant-totality UPGRADE script, linked in for the same reason as the create script
      above: the totality lock runs the migration a consumer runs, not a paraphrase of it. A
      copy in the test would let the shipped script rot behind a green suite, which is the
      exact gap the create-script link exists to close.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.EventSourcing.SqlServer\Scripts\004_MakeEventTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\004_MakeEventTenantTotal.sql" />
    <!--
      Same rule for the SINGLE-TENANT inbox DDL. The turnkey-composition E2E needs the single-tenant
      shape (PK (MessageId, HandlerType), no TenantId) while the shared inbox fixture is deliberately
      multi-tenant, so the E2E provisions its own database — from the shipped script, not a copy.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Inbox.SqlServer\Scripts\001_CreateInboxSchema.sql" Link="Schema\001_CreateInboxSchema.sql" />
    <!--
      The shipped Postgres OUTBOX schema and its tenant-totality upgrade, linked for the same reason
      as the event-store pair above. The outbox container fixture hand-writes its own DDL — which is
      fine for the store behavioural suites, but means nothing in the tree exercised the script a
      consumer actually runs. The totality lock provisions from these, so a divergence between the
      shipped script and the store surfaces here rather than on a consumer database.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Outbox.Postgres\Scripts\001_CreateOutboxSchema.sql" Link="Schema\001_CreatePostgresOutboxSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Outbox.Postgres\Scripts\002_MakeOutboxTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\002_MakePostgresOutboxTenantTotal.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Outbox.Postgres\Scripts\003_CarryTenantOnDeadLetters.sql" Link="Schema\003_CarryPostgresDeadLetterTenant.sql" />
    <!--
      Migration-drift fixtures. Three renderings of ONE migration id, which is what lets a test
      distinguish an EDITED migration (must be refused) from the SAME migration checked out with
      the other line-ending convention (must be accepted). LogicalName is set explicitly because
      the migrator discovers scripts by resource-name prefix, so these names are the test input
      rather than an artefact of directory layout. Their line endings are pinned in .gitattributes
      and are the SUBJECT of the test, not formatting; the test fails naming that file if the two
      renderings ever become byte-identical, so a regression there cannot pass silently.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Fixtures/MigrationDrift/Original/001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" LogicalName="MigrationDriftProbe.Original.001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Fixtures/MigrationDrift/OriginalCrlf/001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" LogicalName="MigrationDriftProbe.OriginalCrlf.001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Fixtures/MigrationDrift/Edited/001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" LogicalName="MigrationDriftProbe.Edited.001_CreateDriftProbe.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>
